{
  "teamId": 114,
  "year": 2022,
  "narrative": "The 2022 Cleveland Guardians were a testament to resilience and depth, a team that scratched and clawed through an uneven first half before catching fire down the stretch in one of the most electric September runs in recent memory. They entered the season with modest expectations — a young roster, a lean power profile, and a pitching staff that needed to overperform to compensate — and for the first two months, that reality showed in a pedestrian 21-24 record through May.\n\nThe turning point arrived quietly in June and then loudly. Josh Naylor's walk-off two-run home run on June 29th, a swing worth 0.832 WPA — the single most impactful play of Cleveland's regular season — crystallized what the Guardians were becoming: a team that found ways to win in the most pressure-packed moments. Rookies like Oscar Gonzalez stepped forward, timely hits came from unexpected places, and the pitching staff posted a sterling 3.46 ERA that kept Cleveland competitive even on nights when the offense went quiet.\n\nSeptember was where this team wrote its legend. A 21-8 monthly record announced Cleveland's arrival as a genuine division force, and the Guardians rode that wave into October. The Wild Card round against Tampa Bay required all their clutch mettle — José Ramírez homered, Oscar Gonzalez delivered again — and Cleveland punched through to the ALDS. Against the New York Yankees, it was Gonzalez once more who provided the season's most dramatic moment, singling home two runs in the deciding game to keep Cleveland's hopes alive. His single in Game 5 registered a remarkable 0.753 WPA, the kind of hit that defines a season.\n\nUltimately, Giancarlo Stanton's crushing three-run homer off Aaron Civale ended the dream, and the Yankees moved on. But the 2022 Guardians — 92 wins, an AL Central title, and a roster full of young talent — had given Cleveland plenty to believe in. The season wasn't about a single superstar; it was about a collective, a group of grinders who turned a slow start into a September surge and took the sport's most storied franchise to the brink.",
  "chapters": [
